    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a7ba299a-31d9-546b-9ae1-5f8587737816",
      ":question/problem": "Find $m$, if $4{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $m$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 4\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+m\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ m\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ m\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get\n\n$$\n-3m=-24\\,\u27f9\\,m=8.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-24 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $m$ such that $4{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2}=0.$",
